n='nsec-detection'
def q(i,f,s,why='Align quiz with corrected, scoped lesson claim.',src=[]):qedit(n,i,f,s,why,src)
qreplace(n,4,'prompt','Approximately what fraction','Using the expected counts, approximately what fraction','Distinguish expected counts from guaranteed outcomes.')
qreplace(n,4,'workedExample','The detector looks excellent on paper but 99 in 100 alerts are noise. This is the **base-rate fallacy** (Axelsson, 1999). Improving precision (lower FPR, correlation with other signals) usually matters more than catching the last 1% of attacks.','Under these assumed rates, about 99% of alerts are false. This illustrates the base-rate problem: alert precision depends on prevalence as well as sensitivity and false-positive rate. The appropriate trade-off depends on missed-attack and false-alert costs; no universal priority follows from this example.','Remove unverified attribution and unsupported universal operational priority.')
q(5,'options.1.explanation','TLS encrypts application content, not the enclosing TCP ports. Port filtering works when the relevant header is present and supported; later fragments and encapsulation can limit visibility.')
qreplace(n,5,'workedExample','1. Capture filters run in the kernel before packets are saved: fast, limited.\n2. Display filters run over decoded packets: slower, can match any protocol field (`tls.handshake.type == 1`).\n3. Rule of thumb: dots and `==` mean Wireshark display filter.','1. Live capture filters can run in the kernel on supported platforms; offline filtering runs in userspace.\n2. Display filters inspect fields exposed by Wireshark dissectors, such as `tls.handshake.type == 1`. Encrypted or unsupported fields may be unavailable.\n3. Use the correct language for each input box; punctuation alone is not a reliable syntax test.','Kernel placement, performance and available fields are not unconditional; BPF also supports ==.')
q(6,'options.0.explanation','Correct within the observed path: encryption normally leaves packet timing and outer sizes visible, though tunnels, padding and missing observations can obscure application behaviour.')
q(6,'options.2.explanation','Correct as heuristic signals from visible ClientHello features. Fingerprints can collide or be imitated; they do not authenticate the software identity.')
q(6,'options.4.explanation','Incorrect. TLS 1.3 encrypts Certificate messages. Initial certificate-based TLS 1.2 handshakes exposed them, as did earlier versions; resumed handshakes need not send certificates.')
qreplace(n,6,'workedExample','Add DNS logs (often the best signal of all) and flow records. ECH will remove SNI for adopting sites, leaving timing, IPs, DNS and fingerprints.','DNS and flow logs can add evidence within their coverage. ECH conceals the real name in the inner ClientHello; an outer public name can remain visible. Resolver caches and encrypted or unobserved DNS limit name visibility. No signal is universally best.','Scope ECH and DNS visibility and remove unsupported ranking.')
q(7,'options.3.text','It restricts matching to traffic towards the server in a flow that the engine considers established')
q(7,'options.3.explanation','Correct. Flow state and direction constrain matching; they do not cryptographically authenticate the sender or guarantee resistance to forged traffic.')
q(7,'workedExample','For ordinary tracked TCP, established state follows the handshake; midstream pickup can alter what was observed. The to_server direction excludes server-to-client responses. For other protocols, established state can have another meaning, such as observing both directions. This is a matching constraint, not a claim that excluded traffic cannot be an attack.','Established flow semantics vary by protocol and capture configuration.',['https://docs.suricata.io/en/latest/rules/flow-keywords.html'])
q(8,'prompt','Assume captured packet bytes average 2 decimal Gbps continuously. Ignoring capture-file overhead, compression and replication, roughly how much storage is needed for 30 days?')
qreplace(n,8,'workedExample','That is why full capture is usually kept for hours or a few days, or triggered on alerts, while flow records (bytes per *connection*, not per packet) are kept for months.','This is a packet-byte-rate model, not an exact conversion from physical link utilization: framing and gaps consume link capacity but are not all stored. File metadata and replication can add storage, while sampling, truncation or compression can reduce it. Retention must be budgeted for the actual system.','Separate arithmetic from unsupported retention prevalence and incorrect flow record granularity.')
q(9,'prompt','A Linux host capture shows outbound TCP buffers carrying 28,960 payload bytes with apparent bad checksums on a link with a 1,500-byte IP MTU. What is a likely explanation to investigate first?')
q(9,'options.0.explanation','With segmentation offload, the captured buffer can exceed the IP MTU while resulting wire IP packets respect it. Ethernet frames additionally include link-layer headers and trailers.')
q(9,'options.1.explanation','Correct as a likely offload artifact. Compare a suitable external capture and the offload configuration before concluding that malformed packets went onto the wire.')
q(9,'options.2.explanation','This pattern is consistent with offload artifacts; it does not by itself establish tampering or exclude every other cause.')
q(9,'options.3.explanation','A capture-file problem is possible in general, but large transmit buffers and incomplete checksums specifically suggest offload effects.')
q(9,'workedExample','Assume 20-byte IPv4 and 32-byte TCP headers, so an IP MTU of 1,500 permits 1,448 payload bytes per segment. A buffer of 20 × 1,448 = 28,960 payload bytes can be split into 20 packets. A capture before transmit offload completes can show the large buffer and unfinished checksums. Receive coalescing can cause large apparent packets at some capture hooks too. Actual visibility depends on the operating system, driver, offloads and capture point.')
q(10,'options.0.text','The action field reports acceptance or rejection, but does not uniquely identify the rejecting rule')
q(10,'options.0.explanation','Correct. REJECT may reflect security-group or NACL rules, or packets arriving after connection closure.',['Scope AWS action semantics.'][0],['https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpc/latest/userguide/flow-log-records.html'])
q(10,'options.2.explanation','Correct. The default maximum is 10 minutes with a 1-minute option; Nitro interfaces always use at most 1 minute. Publication adds further delay.')
qreplace(n,10,'workedExample','Typical exfiltration query: group by `dstaddr`, sum `bytes`, filter for non-corporate destinations, sort descending, and compare with the instance\'s normal baseline.','For one observation point and appropriate egress records, group by destination and compare byte totals with a baseline. Account for translated addresses, duplicate observations and missing records. Large volume alone does not establish exfiltration; SKIPDATA and documented exclusions limit completeness.','Scope aggregate inference and coverage.')
qreplace(n,11,'workedExample','Distinct targets per source catch both vertical scans (many ports, one host) and horizontal scans (one port, many hosts).','Distinct target pairs can flag both vertical probing (many ports, one host) and horizontal probing (one port, many hosts). Thresholding can miss slow or distributed scans and can flag legitimate activity.','Heuristic detection does not guarantee catching all scan styles.')
